":"A shared table of zero-suppressed decision diagrams: families of sets drawn from the elements","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0 ..< elementCount"},{"text":", each a directed acyclic graph whose vertices test one element and branch","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to a low child (the sets without it) and a high child (the sets with it, the element removed),","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"down to the constants: the empty family and the family holding only the empty set (Minato,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"“Zero-Suppressed BDDs for Set Manipulation in Combinatorial Problems”, 1993).","type":"text"}],"relationshipsSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/s16SendableMetatypeP"],"kind":"relationships","type":"conformsTo","title":"Conforms To"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/documentation\/DecisionDiagrams\/ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram"},"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Elements are tested in index order, 0 nearest the root. The table never holds two vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that test the same element with the same children, nor a vertex whose high child is the empty"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"family, so each family has one vertex: two "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/documentation\/DecisionDiagrams\/ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram\/Family"},{"type":"text","text":" values are equal exactly when they hold"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the same sets. An element no set of a family contains never appears in its diagram, which keeps"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the diagrams of sparse families small."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["var diagram = ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram(elementCount: 3)","let f = diagram.family([[0, 1], [1, 2], [2]])","diagram.count(of: f)                                  \/\/ 3","let g = diagram.including(2, in: f)                   \/\/ {{1, 2}, {2}}","diagram.minimalSets(of: g) == diagram.family([[2]])   \/\/ true","diagram.join(diagram.single(0), g)                    \/\/ {{0, 1, 2}, {0, 2}}"]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Families are built by the mutating methods, which add vertices to the table and leave every"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"existing family as it was; a family belongs to the table it was built in and its copies. The"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"table keeps every vertex it has made."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"references":{"doc://DecisionDiagrams/documentation/DecisionDiagrams":{"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/decisiondiagrams","title":"DecisionDiagrams","identifier":"doc:\/\/DecisionDiagrams\/documentation\/DecisionDiagrams","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Canonical diagrams of Boolean functions and families of sets."}],"role":"collection"},"doc://DecisionDiagrams/documentation/DecisionDiagrams/ZeroSuppressedDecisionDiagram/vertexCount(of:)":{"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/decisiondiagrams\/zerosuppresseddecisiondiagram\/vertexcount(of:)","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The number of vertices in "},{"code":"f","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"’s diagram, the constants it reaches included."}],"title":"vertexCount(of:)","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kin